Slippery pavements, people sneezing;  
Lords in ermine, beggars freezing;  
Titled gluttons carving dainties;  
Genius starving in a garret.  

Lofty mansions, warm and spacious;  
Courtiers cringing and voracious;  
Misers scarcely heeding the wretched;  
Gallant soldiers fighting, bleeding.  

Wives who laugh at passive spouses;  
Theatres and meeting-houses;  
Balls where simpering misses languish;  
Hospitals and groans of anguish.  

Arts and sciences bewailing;  
Commerce drooping, credit failing;  
Placemen mocking loyal subjects;  
Separations, royal weddings.  

Authors who cannot earn a dinner;  
Many a subtle rogue a winner;  
Fugitives seeking shelter;  
Misers hoarding, tradesmen breaking.  

Taste and talent quite deserted;  
All the laws of truth perverted;  
Arrogance soaring over merit;  
Merit silently deploring.  

Ladies gambling night and morning;  
Fools scorning the works of genius;  
Ancient dames mistaken for girls;  
Youthful damsels quite forsaken.  

Some delighting in luxury;  
More inclined to talking than fighting;  
Aged lovers and decrepit beaux;  
Empty, insipid lordlings.  

Poets, painters, and musicians;  
Lawyers, doctors, politicians;  
Pamphlets, newspapers, and odes,  
All seeking fame by different roads.  

Gallant souls with empty purses;  
Generals fit only for nurses;  
Schoolboys, smitten with martial spirit,  
Taking the place of veteran merit.  

Honest men who cannot obtain places;  
Knaves displaying unblushing faces;  
Ruin hastened, peace retarded;  
Candor spurned, and art rewarded.

outputs.L2:
Slick streets underfoot, people caught in fits of sneezing;  
Nobles wrapped in ermine, while beggars shiver in the cold.  
High-born gourmands slice into delicacies,  
As genius wastes away hungry in an attic room.  

Grand houses rise, roomy and heated;  
Court favorites bow and scrape, greedy for more.  
Misers barely notice the suffering poor;  
Brave soldiers struggle in battle, wounded and bleeding.  

Wives mock husbands who quietly submit;  
There are playhouses and houses of worship.  
At dances, coy young ladies pine and pose;  
In hospitals, pain is heard in groans and cries.  

The arts and sciences lament their condition;  
Trade declines, confidence collapses.  
Office-holders ridicule faithful subjects;  
Royal partings occur alongside royal marriages.  

Writers cannot make enough to buy a meal;  
Many a clever scoundrel comes out ahead.  
Runaways search for refuge;  
Misers pile up wealth while shopkeepers go bankrupt.  

Taste and ability are entirely neglected;  
Every law of honesty is twisted.  
Pride rises above true worth,  
While merit grieves in silence.  

Women of fashion gamble from morning until night;  
Fools sneer at works of genius.  
Elderly ladies are mistaken for young girls,  
While truly young women are ignored.  

Some live only for luxury;  
More are bold in speech than in combat.  
Aged lovers and worn-out dandies appear,  
Along with shallow, flavorless little lords.  

Poets, painters, and composers stand among them;  
So do lawyers, physicians, and statesmen.  
Pamphlets, journals, and poems all circulate,  
Each pursuing fame by its own path.  

Brave spirits possess empty purses;  
Some generals seem fit only for the nursery.  
Schoolboys, seized by military enthusiasm,  
Are preferred over seasoned veterans of real merit.  

Honest men cannot obtain appointments,  
While rogues show their faces without shame.  
Destruction is hurried on, peace is delayed;  
Sincerity is rejected, and cunning receives the prize.

counterfactuals.output:
**A Broadside for the Times**  
*To be cried in the streets, and pasted on every post*

The miser locks his iron chest, and starves beside the key;  
The beggar warms his naked hands, and sings more merrily.

The courtier bows till backbone cracks, yet calls his bending grace;  
He loses all his honest height to gain a painted place.

The placeman eats his country’s bread, and swears the loaf is small;  
He fattens on the public want, and thanks himself for all.

My Lady stakes her diamonds down, and laughs to lose a year;  
Her footman’s wages wait unpaid, her lapdog’s coach is near.

Lord Limpet lisps a borrowed jest, and thinks the town has shone;  
His tailor made him half a man, his title made the bone.

Sir Dulness buys a gallery full, and never sees a face;  
He hangs dead masters on his walls, and banishes live grace.

The parson thunders temperance out, then dines till candles swim;  
He damns the flesh in morning zeal, and sups the world at dim.

The lawyer splits a widow’s mite, and pockets both the halves;  
He weeps at Mercy in the court, then charges for his calves.

The merchant cries that trade is sick, with warehouses in bloom;  
He wears a famine on his tongue, and velvet in his room.

The patriot roars of liberty, when out of office cast;  
Give him a chair, his chains turn gold, and principles go fast.

The soldier curses foreign pride, yet struts in borrowed lace;  
He wins a scar upon his shin, and calls it deathless grace.

The beau perfumes his empty skull, and powders what he lacks;  
His head is all a dressing-room, his heart a box of wax.

The critic prunes Apollo’s bays with microscopic spite;  
He cannot kindle one poor spark, but snuffs a thousand lights.

The pamphleteer sells wrath by sheet, and conscience by the ream;  
His ink is gall on Monday morn, on Tuesday courtier-cream.

The banker sighs at hardening times, and clips the poor man’s breath;  
He lends a rope at interest, then forecloses after death.

The alderman laments excess, with turtle at his chin;  
He votes for public virtue—then lets private pudding in.

The wit grows tame at supper-boards, where fools can pay the fee;  
He barters lightning for a place, and calls the thunder free.

Neglected Genius climbs the stair, and knocks with frozen hand;  
Dunce dines below on silver plate, and never understands.

The poet warms his noble rage above a candle-end;  
The blockhead gets a pension signed, because he knows a friend.

Thus Vice goes coach’d with six before, and Merit limps behind;  
The age has spectacles for wealth, and blinks when worth is blind.

Come, neighbours, read this faithful sheet, then laugh, and homeward creep;  
We mock the sins we mean to keep, and wake—to fall asleep.
